(:cartridge :type :data :id "oak-data-fractions-decimals" :title "Fractions, Decimals & Percentages" :stage "Key Stage 2" :subject "Mathematics" :unit "Fractions and Decimals"
  (:axiom "A fraction represents equal parts of a whole; multiplying or dividing both numerator and denominator by the same non-zero number produces an equivalent fraction.")
  (:trap "Adding denominators together when adding fractions (e.g. 1/3 + 1/3 = 2/6), or thinking 0.45 is larger than 0.5 because 45 > 5.")
  (:socratic-pivot "If you cut a pizza into 4 slices and eat 2, is that the same amount as cutting it into 8 slices and eating 4?")
  (:hook "Sharing chocolate bars fairly among friends without leaving crumb remainders.")
  (:guided-step "Align denominators using lowest common multiples before attempting addition or subtraction.")
  (:questions (
    (:q :id "fd-1" :prompt "Which fraction is equivalent to 3/4?"
        :options ("9/12" "6/10" "4/5" "7/8")
        :answer 0
        :tier "recall"
        :hint "Multiply both the numerator (3) and the denominator (4) by the same integer."
        :explanation "Multiplying top and bottom by 3 yields (3 × 3) / (4 × 3) = 9/12. Both express the exact same mathematical value (0.75)."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! Multiplying numerator and denominator by 3 gives 9/12." "Misconception: 6/10 simplifies to 3/5, not 3/4." "Misconception: Adding 1 to numerator and denominator changes the fraction value (3/4 = 0.75, 4/5 = 0.8)." "Misconception: Adding 4 to both numbers changes the proportion (7/8 = 0.875).")
    )
    (:q :id "fd-2" :prompt "What is 2/5 + 1/10 expressed in its simplest form?"
        :options ("1/2" "3/15" "3/10" "3/5")
        :answer 0
        :tier "application"
        :hint "Convert 2/5 to tenths first so both fractions have a common denominator."
        :explanation "2/5 = 4/10. Adding 4/10 + 1/10 gives 5/10, which simplifies by dividing top and bottom by 5 to equal 1/2."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! 4/10 + 1/10 = 5/10 = 1/2." "Misconception: You must never add denominators together (2+1)/(5+10) = 3/15 is mathematically invalid." "Misconception: 2/5 was forgotten or treated as 2/10 instead of converting to 4/10." "Misconception: 1/10 was overlooked during common denominator alignment.")
    )
    (:q :id "fd-3" :prompt "Which decimal represents the exact value of the fraction 7/100?"
        :options ("0.07" "0.7" "0.70" "7.0")
        :answer 0
        :tier "recall"
        :hint "Seven hundredths occupies the second digit after the decimal point."
        :explanation "The first place after the decimal point is tenths; the second is hundredths. 7 hundredths is written as 0.07."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! 7 hundredths is 0.07." "Misconception: 0.7 is 7 tenths (7/10), which is 10 times larger than 7/100." "Misconception: 0.70 is also 7 tenths (70/100 = 7/10)." "Misconception: 7.0 is seven whole units.")
    )
    (:q :id "fd-4" :prompt "Which comparison statement between 0.45 and 0.5 is correct?"
        :options ("0.5 is greater than 0.45 because 0.5 is equal to 0.50 (50 hundredths > 45 hundredths)" "0.45 is greater than 0.5 because 45 is a larger number than 5" "They are equal because 0.5 and 0.45 both round to 0" "0.45 is greater because it has more digits after the decimal point")
        :answer 0
        :tier "analysis"
        :hint "Pad 0.5 with a zero to make it 0.50, then compare the hundredths."
        :explanation "Looking at place value: 0.5 has 5 tenths (0.50 = 50 hundredths), whereas 0.45 only has 4 tenths and 5 hundredths (45 hundredths). Therefore, 0.5 > 0.45."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! Comparing digits by place value shows 5 tenths exceeds 4 tenths." "Misconception: Whole-number thinking trap (believing longer decimals are automatically larger numbers)." "Misconception: Decimal values are continuous numbers, not rounded approximations." "Misconception: Number of digits indicates precision, not magnitude.")
    )
    (:q :id "fd-5" :prompt "What is 3/8 of 48?"
        :options ("18" "16" "24" "6")
        :answer 0
        :tier "application"
        :hint "Divide 48 by the denominator (8) to find 1/8, then multiply by the numerator (3)."
        :explanation "First find one eighth: 48 ÷ 8 = 6. Then multiply by three: 6 × 3 = 18."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! (48 ÷ 8) × 3 = 6 × 3 = 18." "Misconception: 48 ÷ 3 = 16, which calculates 1/3 of 48 instead of 3/8." "Misconception: 24 is half of 48 (4/8)." "Misconception: 6 is only 1/8 of 48; you still need to multiply by 3.")
    )
    (:q :id "fd-6" :prompt "A shirt originally costing £40 is discounted by 25%. What is the sale price?"
        :options ("£30" "£35" "£10" "£25")
        :answer 0
        :tier "application"
        :hint "25% is equivalent to 1/4. Find 1/4 of £40, then subtract it from £40."
        :explanation "25% = 1/4. One quarter of £40 is £10. Subtracting the discount (£40 - £10) gives the sale price of £30."
        :misconceptions ("Correct! £40 - (25% of £40) = £40 - £10 = £30." "Misconception: Subtracting £5 rather than 25%." "Misconception: £10 is the amount of the discount, not the final price." "Misconception: Confusing the percentage value with the price amount.")
    )
  ))
)
